    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p27-true-false-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "To prove a binomial identity with a combinatorial argument, it is enough to count the same set in two different ways.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Both expressions count the same set of outcomes, so they are equal.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-true-false-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Choosing a president and a vice-president is the same as first choosing two people and then deciding which is president.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Once two people are chosen, choosing which is president fixes the other as vice-president. Both procedures select the same two people with the same designated offices.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-true-false-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Appointing a captain first and then choosing ten people to join the captain is the same as choosing eleven team members because they’ll end up deciding who the captain is anyway.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. Choosing eleven members alone does not designate a captain.",
          "worked": "False. The first procedure selects an eleven-person team with a designated captain. Choosing eleven members alone selects only the team. To count the same outcomes, the second procedure must also choose one of those eleven members as captain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-true-false-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Choosing who to allow inside is the same as choosing who to keep outside.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Choosing the people inside uniquely determines the complementary set outside, and choosing the people outside uniquely determines those inside.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-true-false-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "The number of ways to choose a committee of size $k$ and a separate observer from the same $n$ people is the same as choosing an observer and then forming a committee of size $k-1$ from the $n-1$ remaining people.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. The committee must still have $k$ members.",
          "worked": "False. The observer is separate from the committee. After choosing the observer, choose $k$ committee members from the remaining $n-1$ people, giving $n\\binom{n-1}{k}$ choices. Choosing only $k-1$ committee members counts a different selection.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
